<p>Section 07</p>
<h2>Colour &amp; Light in Scandinavian Interiors for Indian Homes</h2>
<p>Colour is where Scandinavian design is most misread in India. The assumption is that Scandi means pure white everywhere. In reality, the Nordic palette is a carefully layered range of warm neutrals, nature-inspired soft tones and strategic dark accents — all calibrated to the quality of Northern European light. In India, that calibration needs adjustment.</p>
<h3>The Scandi-India Colour Palette</h3>
<p>Warm White <br>#F9F6F1</p>
<p>Linen <br>#EDE8E0</p>
<p>Warm Sand <br>#D4C9B4</p>
<p>Sage Grey <br>#B5BDB5</p>
<p>Muted Sage <br>#7E9A8A</p>
<p>Slate Blue <br>#4A5759</p>
<p>Charcoal <br>#2D2B28</p>
<p>Warm Oak <br>#C4A882</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Critical India Adaptation</span>Avoid pure brilliant white (#FFFFFF or similar) for walls in Indian homes. Under tropical daylight, it creates a glare that feels clinical and harsh. Choose warm whites with a yellow-red undertone (think Dulux White on White, Asian Paints Milky White, or Berger WhiteOut) that feel bright but never harsh. Assess the sample in your actual room at noon and at 6pm — the same colour reads very differently at those two times in Indian daylight.</div>
<h3>Using Dark Accents the Scandi Way</h3>
<p>One of the most powerful and least understood aspects of Scandinavian design is the use of deep charcoal, forest green, navy or matte black as accent tones against the dominant light palette. In Indian homes, these accents are almost always the element that elevates a Scandi space from pleasant to sophisticated.</p>
<p>A charcoal-painted interior door against white walls. A matte black kitchen tap against white cabinets. A deep slate-blue gallery wall in the study behind white bookshelves. A single dark-stained oak wardrobe in an otherwise all-white bedroom. These contrasts create depth, drama and visual interest without disrupting the calm of the overall palette.</p>
