<p>Section 08</p>
<h2>Room-by-Room Scandinavian Design Guide</h2>
<p>The Scandi living room is the home's social and emotional core — the space designed explicitly for gathering, conversation, reading and the daily practice of Hygge. In an Indian home, it must also flex to accommodate extended family visits, festival celebrations and the informal entertaining that is central to Indian domestic culture.</p>
<p>Sofa Style</p>
<p>Low-profile, modular, neutral</p>
<p>Rug</p>
<p>Dhurrie, jute or wool flatweave</p>
<p>Coffee Table</p>
<p>Light oak or white concrete</p>
<p>Light Fixture</p>
<p>Rattan pendant or Edison cluster</p>
<p>The Scandi living room is anchored by a low, large sofa in a muted linen, dusty grey or warm white fabric — not a leather three-seater, which reads as too formal and heavy. A Dhurrie or flatweave jute rug defines the seating zone and introduces an Indian craft element that integrates beautifully with the Nordic aesthetic. The coffee table is kept deliberately low and simple — light ash or bleached oak on tapered legs.</p>
<p>The most distinctive element in a Scandi Indian living room is the gallery wall — not a chaotic mix of family photographs, but a curated grid of 8–12 simple black or natural wood frames containing: a botanical print, a piece of Indian folk art (Madhubani, Warli or Gond), an abstract line drawing, a world map, and two or three personal photographs. This gallery wall becomes the room's narrative — a thoughtful, characterful focal point that tells the story of who lives there.</p>
<p>The Scandi kitchen is designed around the idea of cooking as a pleasure and a social act — not a utility hidden behind a closed door. Open shelving displaying beautiful ceramics, a simple colour scheme in white or light grey, and integrated appliances give the Scandi kitchen its distinctive look. Adapting this for Indian cooking requires honest thinking about your daily cooking habits.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The Indian Kitchen Reality Check</span>Pure open shelving — a hallmark of Nordic kitchens in design magazines — is impractical for active Indian cooking environments. Daily oil splatter and steam from tadka, sabzi and roti-making covers open shelves in grease within weeks. The solution: use open shelving only above the breakfast counter or pantry zone, and reserve full closed cabinets for the cooking zone. This hybrid approach maintains the Scandi aesthetic in the areas it shows, while remaining practical where it matters.</div>
