</ul>
<h4>Where to Be Smart</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Rugs — excellent quality from Indian weaver cooperatives at fraction of Nordic prices</li>
  <li>Baskets and storage — Indian rattan and jute artisans are world-class</li>
  <li>Art — local art school prints, digital downloads printed locally in quality frames</li>
  <li>Plants — local nurseries are far cheaper and plants acclimatise better</li>
  <li>Side tables and small furniture — IKEA India + Indian carpenter customisation</li>
  <li>Cushion covers — handloom cooperatives and block-print studios across India</li>
</ul>
<p>Section 14</p>
<h2>How to Choose the Right Designer for a Scandi Interior in India</h2>
<p>Scandinavian design requires a designer with a specific sensibility — someone who is comfortable with restraint, who does not try to fill every wall, who can specify materials with the same rigour that a Nordic firm would. Many talented Indian interior designers are not well-suited to Scandi work because their design instincts lean toward the more maximalist Indian market mainstream. Here is how to evaluate.</p>
<h4>Ask to See Their Scandi or Minimalist Portfolio Specifically</h4>
<p>Many designers can produce excellent traditional or contemporary Indian interiors but have never executed a genuinely spare, Nordic-influenced project. Ask for three examples of minimal, light-toned interiors they have completed in real Indian homes. CGI renders are not evidence. Completed projects with client testimonials are.</p>
<h4>Test Their Material Knowledge</h4>
<p>Ask what oak veneer brand or supplier they use, what their position on linen upholstery in Indian conditions is, and what LED colour temperature they specify by default. A designer who answers these questions fluently and specifically has done this before. Vague answers suggest they are designing from aesthetics alone, without material expertise.</p>
<h4>Check Their Lighting Specification Process</h4>
<p>Scandi interiors live or die on lighting. Ask whether they produce a lighting design plan — a separate document specifying every fitting, its colour temperature, its placement, its beam angle and whether it is on a dimmer. If they do not produce a dedicated lighting plan, the lighting will be an afterthought.</p>
<h4>Discuss the Pooja Room and Traditional Elements Upfront</h4>
<p>A good Scandi designer in the Indian context will have a point of view on how to integrate a pooja room, heirloom furniture or traditional objects into a Nordic scheme — not simply refuse or ignore them. If a designer cannot articulate how they would handle these elements, they lack the Indian interior context necessary for the project.</p>
<h4>Insist on a Phased Payment Milestone Structure</h4>
<p>Never pay more than 30–35% upfront for any interior project. A standard payment structure should be: 30% at design sign-off, 40% at material delivery to site, 20% at substantial completion, 10% at final handover after a snag walkthrough. Any deviation from this structure is a risk signal.</p>
